# The maturity label, and its exact scope

Every other system measured here is PROTOTYPE-RESEARCH and says so in its own README.
gnark does not, and the difference is not a matter of tone: it lists **nine third-party
audits**, five of them vendored in `audits/` in the tree we measured.

    2022-10  Kudelski Security      gnark-crypto              (contracted by Algorand Fdn)
    2023-06  Consensys Diligence    gnark PLONK Solidity verifier
    2023-08  Least Authority        Groth16 Solidity verifier template  (Worldcoin)
    2023-11  OpenZeppelin           gnark PLONK Solidity verifier template
    2024-05  Sigma Prime            gnark-crypto KZG          (contracted by Ethereum Fdn)
    2024-05  ZKSecurity.xyz         gnark standard library
    2024-06  OpenZeppelin           gnark PLONK prover and verifier
    2024-09  Least Authority        gnark general and GKR
    2024-11  Least Authority        Linea zkEVM

**Scope, stated precisely rather than rounded up.** The PLONK *prover and verifier* were
audited (OpenZeppelin, 2024-06). For Groth16, what appears in the list is the **Solidity
verifier template**, not the Go prover. The standard library — which is where
`std/rangecheck`, the mechanism this benchmark's INT8 encoding rests on, lives — was audited
(ZKSecurity, 2024-05). We did not read the reports and we do not restate their findings.

The authors' own caveat, verbatim from README.md line 124, and it governs the label:

    **`gnark` and [`gnark-crypto`] have been [extensively audited](#audits), but are provided
    as-is with no guarantees or warranties. In particular, `gnark` does not guarantee
    constant-time implementations or side-channel resistance.**

README.md line 66, which bears directly on the proof-size and corruption figures:

    - Serialized formats are not guaranteed to be stable across versions.

# The licence permits what DeepProve's and jolt-atlas's do not

Apache-2.0. As with binius64 and Ceno, we may read the source, link the packages, instrument
them and publish the mechanism. Three things in this directory exist only because of that:

1. `RESULTS.md` publishes a **complete byte map of the Groth16 proof**, field by field,
   derived from `backend/groth16/bn254/marshal.go`. Every accepted offset in the corruption
   sweep is therefore attributable to a named field. jolt-atlas had two accepted regions and
   could only name one; the other stayed NOT DETERMINED because its licence forbids the
   reverse engineering that would have named it.
2. `EXPRESSION.md` cites `std/rangecheck`'s selection logic to explain *why* the per-value
   range-check cost is not a constant, rather than reporting that it moved and declining to
   say why.
3. `BUILD.md` states which assembly this machine does and does not get, file by file.

**This asymmetry is a licence difference, not an opacity difference.**

# The tree is NOT vendored into this repository

The clone lives at `~/Claude/ext-bench/gnark` and is not committed here. `COMMIT` plus
`bench/scripts/gnark/` reconstruct it from zero. The task circuits under
`bench/tasks/gnark/` are ours and are committed; they depend on gnark as a normal Go module
at the pinned version, so no copy of gnark's source is redistributed.

